INT. GRAIN SILO FREIGHT ELEVATOR - NIGHT

A rusted cage elevator, open at the sides, hangs in the cavernous dark of a silo. A single caged bulb swings overhead. Below, forty meters down, a drift of grain glimmers like gold sand.

VALERIAN ASHFORD-OKORO, a lean man in his fifties with a gray ponytail and an unbuttoned coat, grips the control lever. Across from him stands DOROTHEA KLEIN-AMADI, a woman in her forties in a flight jacket, her back pressed to the cage and her eyes on his hands. The elevator hums, mid-shaft, motionless.

She breathes slowly. He does not look at her.

VALERIAN
Press the button.

DOROTHEA
Which one?

VALERIAN
The red one.

She doesn't move. He sighs.

VALERIAN (CONT'D)
It won't hurt you.

DOROTHEA
It's the alarm.

VALERIAN
It's the stop.

DOROTHEA
It says ALARM.

VALERIAN
The label is wrong.

She laughs, shrill and brittle. He turns his head. In the swinging light, his eyes are red-rimmed and exhausted.

VALERIAN (CONT'D)
Press it.

DOROTHEA
Why do you need it stopped?

VALERIAN
So we can talk.

DOROTHEA
We are talking.

VALERIAN
So we can talk without the sound.

She studies him. Then slowly, she lifts a gloved hand and presses the red button. A shriek tears through the silo. He winces. She holds it. The cage shudders and jerks, and her face is a mask of calm.

He lunges. She drops the glove and slams him against the wall. For a second they are locked together, breathing in each other's faces, and then she shoves him back, hard, and the lever snaps under her palm. The elevator drops, ten feet, twenty, and catches with a bang that rattles every bone.

They hang there, gasping. The alarm dies. The bulb swings. In the new silence, a faint trickle of grain begins to run.

DOROTHEA
(panting)
That's the stop.

VALERIAN
(gasping)
That's the stop.

He slides down the wall to sit. She stays upright, trembling.

DOROTHEA
Now talk.

VALERIAN
You won't like it.

DOROTHEA
I have never liked you.

He laughs. It becomes a cough. She waits.

VALERIAN
I sold the shipment.

DOROTHEA
Which shipment?

VALERIAN
All of it.

She stops breathing. Looks at the dark below. The grain has found a seam and is pouring, a thin gold thread into the black.

DOROTHEA
Who bought it?

VALERIAN
You don't want to know.

DOROTHEA
Valerian.

VALERIAN
Your brother.

The bulb stops swinging. The elevator creaks. She closes her eyes. When she opens them, her face is clear and cold and very quiet.

DOROTHEA
Good.

VALERIAN
Good?

DOROTHEA
I was hoping it was him.

She steps over his legs, grabs the broken lever, and wrenches it back into the housing. The motor groans. The elevator begins, slowly, to rise.

VALERIAN
Where are we going?

DOROTHEA
To see my brother.

VALERIAN
And me?

She looks down at him. Ignores the question. Rolls her shoulders. The cage climbs toward the dim circle of the roof, grain still whispering through the dark below.
